# The circle tour: one full CCW circle, driven as eight arc twists.
#
# SIZING (2026-09-01). r = 300 mm. A circle driven CCW from a point on
# its own circumference puts its centre 90 deg to the left, so it spans
# +-r in x and 0..2r in y from the start: 600 x 600 mm. Against the
# working budget of +-550 x +-350 mm (tours/FIELD.md) that is the
# largest round radius that fits the tight axis. Stage the robot at
# (0, -300) relative to the field centre so the circle sits centred.
#
# The earlier 150 mm radius was sized against a smaller usable area and
# left half the field unused; before that, 250 mm was sized against the
# FULL field rather than the usable part and drove the robot to
# (182, 376) mm, after which the next tour failed against the north
# rail.
#
# ARC SEGMENTS ARE 45 DEG AND THAT IS NOT COSMETIC. moveX() splits any
# move with a nonzero distance and |rotation| >= 50 deg into a pivot
# THEN a straight, so the original single 360 deg arc drove the whole
# circle as one 942 mm STRAIGHT LINE (MEASURED gopiv 2026-09-01,
# closure 942.2 mm), and a 90 deg version drew a square. Eight 45 deg
# arcs keep every segment a genuine constant-curvature move.
#
#   v = omega * r, with omega = 47.75 deg/s = 0.8333 rad/s
#   -> v = 0.8333 * 300 = 250 mm/s, matching the other tours' cruise.

# PIVOT TUNING (MEASURED gopiv 2026-09-01, interleaved A/B so battery
# drift hits both arms equally). Pivot cruise 188 mm/s (omega 180 deg/s)
# beats 300 mm/s (286 deg/s) decisively: excess +1.34 deg sd 0.35 versus
# +6.88 deg sd 1.03, n=8 each. pivot_overrun was then swept AT 188 mm/s
# and 0.68 -- the value the closure sweep found at 300 mm/s -- leaves
# +1.44 deg on the table; 2.0 is the zero crossing (1.8 -> +0.29,
# 2.2 -> -0.25). pivot_overrun is speed-dependent: retune it whenever
# the pivot cruise changes.

SET twist_hold_gain 8
SET speed_floor 512
SET yaw_taper 800
SET pivot_overrun 2.0

REPEAT 8

MARK circle
TWIST vx=250 omega=47.75 angle=45 timeout=6

END
STOP dwell=0.5
